<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tarqui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1829">1829</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Portete de Tarqui · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-colombia-peru-war">Gran Colombia–Peru War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tarqui was fought on 27 February 1829 at Portete de Tarqui near Cuenca in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. Gran Colombian forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-flores">Juan José Flores</a> defeated the Peruvian army of President José de La Mar. The victory saved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> for Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> and led to a peace treaty later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tarqui.jpg" alt="Battle of Tarqui" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jefferson · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla-de-tarqui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Relations between Gran Colombia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> had soured after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s rule in Peru, which ended when he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1826. Peruvian leaders distrusted his plans for a federation of Peru and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, while Bolívar's supporters revived the dispute over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumbes">Tumbes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a> and Maynas. The spark was Peru's part in the overthrow of the pro-Bolívar government of Bolivia in mid-1828, after which the Congress of Peru authorised President José de La Mar, himself a native of Cuenca, to march against the Gran Colombian army.</p><p>A Peruvian naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> closed the Gran Colombian Pacific coast and ended with the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> on 1 February 1829. La Mar had entered Colombian territory on 28 November 1828, occupying Loja and most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Azuay. Antonio José de Sucre, back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> after resigning the Bolivian presidency, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-flores">Juan José Flores</a>, governor of the department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, gathered an estimated 5,000 troops near Cuenca, facing about 4,500 Peruvian troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In the first part of the battle the Gran Colombian troops destroyed the Peruvian vanguard, with an infantry brigade of three battalions and a cavalry squadron under Colonel José María Camacaro, part of the cavalry brigade of Daniel Florence O'Leary. Later the Peruvian cavalry of the Junín Hussars, led by the Argentine general Mariano Necochea, checked a Colombian counter-charge and forced it back to the line. The Colombian forces suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> losses, and by sunset the Peruvian forces had pulled back to await reinforcements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The generals of both sides signed the Girón Agreement after the battle, but La Mar refused to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, protesting against a column <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre</a> ordered for the battlefield whose inscription boasted that four thousand brave men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> had defeated a Peruvian army of eight thousand. The war ended abruptly when General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-gamarra">Agustín Gamarra</a> overthrew La Mar on 7 June 1829. Gamarra's government signed the Piura Armistice on 10 July, returned Guayaquil on 20 July and concluded the Treaty of Guayaquil on 22 September 1829, which kept the boundaries as they had been before the war.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sucre">Sucre</a> was killed in 1830 on his way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, and Flores became the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>. The Ecuadorian Army keeps the anniversary of the battle as Army Day, and its Presidential Horse Guards Squadron bears the name Tarqui Grenadiers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Tarqui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After independence the republic fought Peru in 1828–1829 over Guayaquil and the southern border provinces; Sucre and Flores defeated the Peruvian army at the <b>Battle of Tarqui</b> in February 1829, but the war deepened the strain on a bankrupt state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
